#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896 :topic/difficulty 0.7206860445301848 :topic/id #uuid "ca183854-66cd-5b1f-9d29-03785c3216b8" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087693 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087500 :question/id #uuid "6587b5f1-8a44-556a-a95e-20c54dfebc04" :question/math-academy-id "e-4367" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix ${A},$ where \n\n$$\n{A}=\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix ${A}$ has $4$ rows and $3$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $4\\times 3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6ae25fc-16eb-598b-a288-742e4b46cb12" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087694 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087501 :question/id #uuid "74a9046e-e0a3-59d6-8b13-437e9a0cf04a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4370" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix ${D},$ where \n\n$$\n{D}\n=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix ${D}$ has $1$ row and $4$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 4.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34fd903a-fa34-57b0-a098-08fa1cbd4f95" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix with One Row or Column"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087511 :question/id #uuid "1fcce090-8441-5547-ab52-18b0ff41ac5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5017" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix?\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1.1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $A$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2 \\times 3.$ $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3 \\times 2.$ $\\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $1$ row and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1 \\times 3.$ $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7286fe-b6e2-5544-8bc6-86afc6cdb813" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087654 :question/id #uuid "5c3b6d53-3dbb-59f6-8d15-a9e6f48927a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9942" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9af4801abdec165de2b720a21dd6a727c578e32cbae9c9c54bd589a1b65043b0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three train stations, where the nodes represent stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents the number of routes between the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}a}$</td><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}b}$</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}c}$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $A,$ and so $a=1.$\n\n- No arrows are starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there are no routes from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=0.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=1.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+0+1 = 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10ca3efc-7d23-5bc1-ab41-f785bed69986" :knowledge-point/title "Matrices for Route Planning"}] :topic/math-academy-id 861 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765}] :topic/title "Introduction to Matrices"}]}
